---
title: Keyboard shortcuts
description: Drive Pau from the keyboard and see how it shares keys with Pi.
order: 2
---

# Keyboard shortcuts

Press `⌘/` on macOS or `Ctrl+/` on Linux to see every shortcut. The list is
also under **Help > Keyboard Shortcuts**. On Linux, read `⌘` as `Ctrl` and
`⌥` as `Alt` in the table below.

| Shortcut                           | Action                                 |
| ---------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| `⌘K`, `⌘⇧P`, `F1`                  | Open the command palette               |
| `⌘O`                               | Open a workspace                       |
| `⌘N`                               | Start a session in this workspace      |
| `Ctrl+Tab`, `Ctrl+Shift+Tab`       | Next or previous session               |
| `⌘1` to `⌘9`                       | Go to the session at that position     |
| `⌥⌘A`                              | Jump to a session that needs attention |
| `Shift+Esc`                        | Clear attention markers                |
| `⌘L`                               | Focus the composer                     |
| `Ctrl+Shift+Up`, `Ctrl+Shift+Down` | Previous or next prompt                |
| `⌘[`, `⌘]`                         | Go back or forward                     |
| `/`                                | Filter the sessions in the bay         |
| `⌥⌘B`                              | Show or hide the workspace column      |
| `` Ctrl+` ``                       | Show or hide the terminal dock         |
| `⌘=`, `⌘-`, `⌘0`                   | Zoom in, zoom out, or reset to 100%    |

Each press of `⌥⌘A` opens the next session that needs attention, in every
view. It visits sessions that wait for you or stopped responding first, then
failed sessions, then sessions that finished a turn while you were elsewhere.
A session you leave alone is not visited again until the jump wraps around.
If the bay's filter hides the next session, the jump clears the filter.

The **File** menu has **New Session** and **Open Workspace**, and the **View**
menu has the zoom commands.

## Command palette

Press `⌘K` or click the search field at the top of the window to open the
command palette. Type part of a name to find an action, session, workspace, or
view, then press `Enter` to run it. Each action shows its shortcut. An action
that the current view cannot run appears dimmed with the reason. Press `Esc` to
close the palette. On Linux, `Ctrl+K` opens the palette everywhere except the
composer and other text fields, where it deletes to the end of the line as in
Pi. Pi keeps `Ctrl+Shift+P` everywhere, so press `F1` while you type.

## Composer keys

The composer uses Pi's keys. If your `keybindings.json` assigns other keys to
one of these Pi actions, the composer uses yours instead.

| Default                  | Pi action              | Action                                     |
| ------------------------ | ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| `Enter`                  | `tui.input.submit`     | Send, or steer the agent during a run      |
| `Shift+Enter`, `Ctrl+J`  | `tui.input.newLine`    | Start a new line                           |
| `Alt+Enter`              | `app.message.followUp` | Queue a follow-up during a run             |
| `Alt+↑`                  | `app.message.dequeue`  | Move queued messages back into the composer |
| `Shift+Tab`              | `app.thinking.cycle`   | Move to the next thinking level            |
| `Ctrl+L`                 | `app.model.select`     | Open the model picker                      |
| `Esc`                    | `app.interrupt`        | Interrupt the run, or stop a shell command |

While a completion list is open, `Esc` closes the list and `Enter` or `Tab`
picks the highlighted item. Press `Esc` again to interrupt the run. When no run
is active, `Esc` stops a running shell command instead. As in Pi,
`↑` at the start of the draft and `↓` at its end browse earlier prompts.

The composer differs from Pi's editor in these ways:

- `⌘Enter` and `Ctrl+Enter` also send, unless your Pi bindings use them.
- Pau changes the thinking level and model only between runs. During a run,
  `Shift+Tab` and `Ctrl+L` explain why instead.
- When the session cannot change its thinking level, `Shift+Tab` moves focus
  to the previous control, as it does in other text fields.
- Text editing keys such as `Ctrl+A`, `Ctrl+K`, and `Alt+B` come from the
  system text field. Pi bindings for `tui.editor` actions do not change them
  or the history keys.
- Paste images with the system paste shortcut, `⌘V` on macOS.
- `Ctrl+O` shows or hides tool output and does not follow `app.tools.expand`.
- Pau has no equivalent for Pi's terminal actions: `Ctrl+C` to clear, `Ctrl+D`
  to exit, `Ctrl+Z` to suspend, and `Ctrl+G` for an external editor. It also
  has none for `Ctrl+P` to cycle models or `Ctrl+T` to fold thinking.

## Slash commands

Type `/` in the composer to list commands. The list shows the commands your
extensions, prompt templates, and skills add, followed by Pi's built-in
commands. Pau runs a built-in itself instead of sending it to the agent:

| Command                   | What Pau does                                          |
| ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |
| `/model [provider/model]` | Switches to the model, or opens the model picker       |
| `/thinking [level]`       | Sets the thinking level, or focuses the level list     |
| `/new`                    | Starts a session in this workspace                     |
| `/resume`                 | Lists the workspace's saved sessions                   |
| `/name [name]`            | Renames the session, or edits its name in the bay      |
| `/session`                | Shows the workspace column                             |
| `/tree`                   | Shows the session tree                                 |
| `/fork`                   | Selects your latest prompt in the session tree         |
| `/clone`                  | Duplicates the session at its current position         |
| `/compact [instructions]` | Compacts the context, with your instructions if given  |
| `/copy`                   | Copies the last reply                                  |
| `/hotkeys`                | Shows the keyboard shortcuts                           |
| `/quit`                   | Stops the session                                      |

From the session tree, choose **Branch from here** to continue from an earlier
prompt, as `/fork` does in Pi.

The other built-ins, such as `/login`, `/settings`, and `/export`, need Pi's
terminal interface. The list shows them as unavailable. To use one, choose
**Open terminal** under the composer, start `pi` in the workspace terminal, and
run the command there. If an extension adds a command with a built-in's name,
Pau sends that command to the extension instead.

## Shell commands

Start a message with `!` to run the rest as a shell command in the workspace,
such as `!ls src`. The transcript shows the command and its output as it
arrives, and the agent sees both with your next prompt. Start with `!!` to keep
the command and its output from the model. Its row says **Not in context**.

Commands run while the agent works too, one at a time. Press `Esc` or choose
**Stop command** to stop one. During a run, the first `Esc` interrupts the run
and the next one stops the command. A `!` or `!!` with nothing after it is sent
as a prompt.

## Pi bindings come first

Pau reads Pi's `keybindings.json` and Pi's defaults. When a Pau shortcut uses
a key that one of your Pi bindings uses, Pi keeps the key where its binding
applies:

- Pi's text-editing bindings, the `tui.editor` and `tui.input` actions, keep
  their keys while the composer or another text field has focus. Elsewhere the
  Pau shortcut still runs.
- Pi's application bindings, such as `app.model.cycleBackward`, keep their keys
  everywhere, and the Pau shortcut stops responding to them.

This mostly affects Linux, where Pi binds many `Ctrl` keys, such as `Ctrl+N`,
`Ctrl+O`, and `Ctrl+K`. If you move a text-editing action to another key in
`keybindings.json`, Pau takes the old key back in text fields too. The
shortcut list names the Pi binding that kept each key and where. The menu
items still work.

## Where shortcuts do not run

Shortcuts do not run while the terminal, a code editor, or a dialog has
focus, so those keys reach the program or the dialog. The one exception is
`` Ctrl+` ``, which hides the terminal dock from inside a docked terminal. In
the composer and other text fields, shortcuts that editing could use, such as
`⌘[` and `⌘L`, do not run either.
